WEBVTT

1
00:00:11.325 --> 00:00:13.166
Pleased to meet you!

2
00:00:13.686 --> 00:00:21.050
My name is Albert Dunbar and I am a 22-year-old actor living and working in London, England, in

3
00:00:21.130 --> 00:00:27.794
1612. Some women who come to see me on stage say that I am the best- looking man

4
00:00:27.834 --> 00:00:30.216
in London, and I tend to agree.

5
00:00:31.548 --> 00:00:37.494
A good actor must pay attention to his appearance at all times, as well as to his speech

6
00:00:37.574 --> 00:00:40.376
and body movement, in order to get the best roles.

7
00:00:41.798 --> 00:00:47.963
Of course I only appear in the most popular productions, especially the plays written by William Shakespeare, the

8
00:00:48.023 --> 00:00:50.045
greatest writer in all of England.

9
00:00:51.386 --> 00:00:56.571
My career began when I was only seven years old and the church organist in my little village

10
00:00:56.611 --> 00:00:57.292
heard me sing.

11
00:00:58.496 --> 00:01:03.900
He immediately told my father that I should audition for the children's choir at the Chapel Royal to

12
00:01:03.940 --> 00:01:04.981
sing for Queen Elizabeth.

13
00:01:06.121 --> 00:01:08.203
Excited, my father took me to St.

14
00:01:08.243 --> 00:01:13.106
James' Palace to audition, and afterward I was invited to be a member of the choir.

15
00:01:15.348 --> 00:01:20.631
It wasn't long before I was acting in plays for the queen too, since boys with high voices

16
00:01:20.691 --> 00:01:22.733
are needed to play all the female roles.

17
00:01:24.034 --> 00:01:26.916
No women are allowed on stage; it just isn't done.

18
00:01:28.232 --> 00:01:33.397
I had lots of fun wearing wigs and playing different parts, but at times there were tense moments

19
00:01:33.797 --> 00:01:36.760
when some of the plays poked fun at the people in the queen's court.

20
00:01:37.881 --> 00:01:43.085
The nobility—the lords and ladies—became very annoyed at the writers and actors.

21
00:01:44.326 --> 00:01:50.512
One time Queen Elizabeth became so angry that everyone in the show was briefly put in jail for

22
00:01:50.592 --> 00:01:51.272
offending her.

23
00:01:52.834 --> 00:01:58.945
When I was 13, Queen Elizabeth died, and I soon joined an acting company associated with the Globe

24
00:01:59.025 --> 00:02:00.926
Theater called the King's Men.

25
00:02:01.987 --> 00:02:07.809
The company members said they liked my pretty looks and my sweet- sounding voice and hired me on

26
00:02:08.049 --> 00:02:08.749
as an apprentice.

27
00:02:09.850 --> 00:02:15.352
But most of all, they liked my ability to read since many young boys in England cannot read

28
00:02:15.552 --> 00:02:16.032
or write.

29
00:02:17.673 --> 00:02:23.175
There were other boys in the company, ranging in age from 10 to 15, and the older actors

30
00:02:23.235 --> 00:02:27.645
taught us how to speak loudly so any large audience would be able to hear us.

31
00:02:29.086 --> 00:02:35.610
We learned the galliard, a dance with quick steps and high jumps, and the canary, a very popular

32
00:02:35.650 --> 00:02:36.691
dance from Spain.

33
00:02:37.831 --> 00:02:41.573
A music teacher taught us how to play the fiddle, lute, and recorder.

34
00:02:43.214 --> 00:02:49.818
Dancing and musicianship are both skills we need for our various roles since the audience always enjoys a

35
00:02:49.918 --> 00:02:50.778
lively song!

36
00:02:52.459 --> 00:02:59.212
But I must tell you, whenever I sing the sad song "Barbara Allen," the theater flows with tears.

37
00:03:00.513 --> 00:03:05.416
All the apprentices also learned how to fight with wooden swords for the fight scenes, which are the

38
00:03:05.456 --> 00:03:07.197
most exciting moments in the theater.

39
00:03:09.439 --> 00:03:12.201
I have come a long way from the choir, I can tell you!

40
00:03:13.902 --> 00:03:19.446
As a boy player, I was given spending money, which I used to buy treats such as oranges

41
00:03:19.506 --> 00:03:20.226
and gingerbread.

42
00:03:21.407 --> 00:03:23.965
I also bought books—I love poetry.

43
00:03:25.847 --> 00:03:31.371
However, we were fined if we were late, missed a rehearsal, or damaged our costumes.

44
00:03:33.293 --> 00:03:38.857
Costumes are the biggest expense for a theater company since they are basically the clothes that people wear

45
00:03:38.917 --> 00:03:40.719
on the very best of occasions.

46
00:03:42.280 --> 00:03:49.566
Staining a richly embroidered doublet or tearing a velvet cape is extremely serious; the Globe acting company spends

47
00:03:49.726 --> 00:03:51.968
hundreds of pounds yearly on costumes.

48
00:03:52.956 --> 00:03:57.657
Since we use very little scenery, elaborate costumes are really important.

49
00:03:59.438 --> 00:04:04.479
When a flag flies from the roof of the theater, people know that there will be a performance

50
00:04:04.659 --> 00:04:05.540
that afternoon.

51
00:04:06.900 --> 00:04:11.881
Now that I am a grown man and famous, an apprentice helps me dress backstage.

52
00:04:13.602 --> 00:04:19.503
Of course, since my voice changed, I no longer play women's parts; now I play the lead male

53
00:04:19.563 --> 00:04:21.504
roles, and I'm excellent at them.

54
00:04:23.084 --> 00:04:28.567
But even though I am a celebrity, I still can't help peeking at the audience through the curtain

55
00:04:28.587 --> 00:04:32.029
like a novice to see how many people have come to see our play.

56
00:04:33.110 --> 00:04:38.292
The groundlings are the poorer folk who must stand on the ground right in front of the stage.

57
00:04:39.573 --> 00:04:41.094
They pay a penny to get in.

58
00:04:42.094 --> 00:04:45.836
The wealthy pay more and have seats on the upper balconies of the theater.

59
00:04:46.837 --> 00:04:52.180
Vendors sell fruit and nuts, and young boys run back and forth delivering bottles of ale.

60
00:04:53.112 --> 00:04:56.835
People converse and some play card games while they wait for the show to begin.

61
00:04:58.496 --> 00:05:05.302
It is very noisy, but when the trumpet blasts three times, people know the play is about to

62
00:05:05.362 --> 00:05:07.604
start and they quiet down expectantly.

63
00:05:10.046 --> 00:05:18.973
Today we are performing Macbeth, a play with a Scottish king, a ghost, three witches, soldiers, and lots

64
00:05:19.153 --> 00:05:19.734
of blood.

65
00:05:21.235 --> 00:05:24.581
People are quite fond of it, and most actors play several parts.

66
00:05:26.482 --> 00:05:30.564
Of course I am playing the leading role of Macbeth.

67
00:05:31.205 --> 00:05:37.588
So when I arrived at the theater this morning, I paced backstage practicing my lines to make sure

68
00:05:37.708 --> 00:05:39.309
I had memorized them all.

69
00:05:40.850 --> 00:05:45.832
There is a man, called a book holder, who holds a copy of the play near the stage

70
00:05:46.132 --> 00:05:48.514
so he can whisper a line if an actor forgets.

71
00:05:49.794 --> 00:05:55.150
But I like to be totally prepared, and I've never needed any prompting.

72
00:05:56.432 --> 00:06:01.036
This morning the stage manager had a difficult time getting the throne in place for the actor who

73
00:06:01.076 --> 00:06:01.777
plays the king.

74
00:06:02.958 --> 00:06:07.943
He also had trouble making the trapdoor work perfectly so the ghost can disappear in act 2.

75
00:06:09.585 --> 00:06:16.011
Some apprentices bustled about before the play, filling pigs' bladders with blood so the sword-fighting scenes would look

76
00:06:16.131 --> 00:06:22.923
real. The actors hide the bladders under their arms and then break them open when another actor pretends

77
00:06:22.983 --> 00:06:23.643
to stab them.

78
00:06:25.244 --> 00:06:28.346
It looks very gory when the blood spills all over the place.

79
00:06:30.167 --> 00:06:32.808
It's a great trick, and audiences love it!

80
00:06:36.250 --> 00:06:42.934
Actors frequently perform on other stages around London, such as the Swan Theater, the Middle Temple Theater, and

81
00:06:42.994 --> 00:06:47.136
the Boar's Head Inn, which the owner renovated so plays could be put on there.

82
00:06:48.032 --> 00:06:53.674
We also travel all over the countryside to do shows in town squares and at local inns.

83
00:06:55.114 --> 00:07:00.296
Last year I was invited to the private estate of an earl to provide the entertainment after supper.

84
00:07:01.736 --> 00:07:07.258
But by the time I made my entrance, a few of the guests were fast asleep and snoring

85
00:07:07.438 --> 00:07:12.499
so loudly that no one could hear me sing my famous version of "Barbara Allen."

86
00:07:14.019 --> 00:07:19.931
I was paid well for my appearance, but I far prefer being at the Globe where my artistic

87
00:07:20.011 --> 00:07:21.873
nature is taken more seriously.

88
00:07:23.114 --> 00:07:24.575
Come see me when you are in London.

89
00:07:25.336 --> 00:07:26.157
Everyone knows me.

90
00:07:27.077 --> 00:07:29.660
Just ask for the great Albert Dunbar!
